const faq = [
  { q: 'What are the main predators of ravens?', a: 'Ravens are preyed upon by large raptors — particularly Golden Eagles (Aquila chrysaetos), Red-tailed Hawks (Buteo jamaicensis), and other large hawks. Great Horned Owls (Bubo virginianus) are significant nocturnal predators of roosting ravens. Ground predators including Red Foxes, martens, and weasels can take eggs, nestlings, and occasionally adults at the nest. Wolves occasionally kill ravens, though the raven-wolf relationship is complex and largely mutualistic. Ravens are rarely preyed upon relative to their size, owing to vigilance, group defence, and mobbing behaviour.' },
  { q: 'How do ravens protect themselves from predators?', a: 'Ravens use several antipredator strategies: group mobbing of raptors and owls (harassing them until they leave); alarm calling to alert conspecifics; nest placement on inaccessible cliff ledges or in tall trees; vigilance — ravens almost always have one or more individuals watching for threats in a group; and their own large size and powerful bill, which makes them capable of physical defence against smaller predators. In communal roosts, the many-eyes effect improves early detection.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens afraid of anything?', a: 'Ravens show wariness toward: large raptors (especially eagles and large hawks), large owls, humans with threatening behaviour or guns, and novel objects in their environment (neophobia). Wild ravens are also wary of traps and unfamiliar objects near food sources — a wariness exploited in research but also a persistent challenge for pest-control operations targeting ravens. Ravens\' caution is not simple fear but involves risk assessment — they will approach danger when reward is sufficient (e.g. food near a carcass where a wolf is feeding).' },
